          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: prōsequor, prōsequī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "prōsequor, prōsequī — \"to escort, to see off, to accompany; to pursue\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Amīcī viātōrem ad portam urbis prōsequuntur. Friends escort the traveller as far as the city gate."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Deponent: prōsequor, prōsequī, prōsecūtus sum. Escorting a departing friend part of the way was a duty. English prosecute and pursue come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "prōsequor is built from prō- 'forward, onward' and sequor 'to follow' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short). English prosecute comes from its participle, and pursue from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
